WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.880
Shine light on certain cells in a mouse's brain, and it freezes.

2
00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.080
Has the memory been found?

3
00:00:10.083 --> 00:00:13.243
The cells were tagged while the mouse learned to fear a chamber.

4
00:00:14.283 --> 00:00:18.123
A gene called c-fos switches on in recently active neurons.

5
00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:24.007
Its promoter can put a light-driven channel, channelrhodopsin-2, into those cells.

6
00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:27.510
A drug, doxycycline, blocks the tag.

7
00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:29.930
Take it away, and a window opens.

8
00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:32.370
Give it back, and the window closes.

9
00:00:33.483 --> 00:00:37.943
Open it around training, and mostly cells active during learning get the channel.

10
00:00:38.933 --> 00:00:42.813
Days later, in a different box, light on the cells made the mice freeze.

11
00:00:43.883 --> 00:00:45.603
Two controls made it specific.

12
00:00:46.763 --> 00:00:49.323
Cells tagged in a neutral box caused no fear freezing.

13
00:00:50.417 --> 00:00:54.497
Mice with a fluorescent protein but no channel did not freeze to light.

14
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.000
So these cells are enough to drive the fear, in this preparation.

15
00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:00.920
That is sufficiency.

16
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.740
Does that mean the mouse needs these cells when it remembers by itself?

17
00:01:11.833 --> 00:01:17.553
Not yet. Necessity needs the reverse: silence the tagged cells during natural

18
00:01:17.553 --> 00:01:19.333
recall, back in the shock box.

19
00:01:20.317 --> 00:01:24.897
Kitamura and colleagues tagged prefrontal cells at learning, then silenced them

20
00:01:24.897 --> 00:01:25.897
later.

21
00:01:26.217 --> 00:01:28.297
On day two, recall was intact.

22
00:01:29.237 --> 00:01:30.817
On day twelve, it was disrupted.

23
00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.300
The standard model predicted that order.

24
00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:36.780
But these cells were tagged at learning.

25
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:42.280
So the cortical part of the memory did not first appear after a later transfer.

26
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.520
It formed at once, and matured.

27
00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:50.020
So in these mice, a memory's group is not fixed in one place.

28
00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:52.500
Where it is depends on when you ask.

29
00:01:53.433 --> 00:01:56.133
That suggests why enough does not prove needed.

30
00:01:56.953 --> 00:02:00.193
If the group spans regions, driving one part can complete it.

31
00:02:01.117 --> 00:02:04.557
Silencing one part may leave enough of the rest to complete it.

32
00:02:05.417 --> 00:02:08.017
All of this is mouse fear, over days to weeks.

33
00:02:08.877 --> 00:02:10.857
It is not yet human memory over years.

34
00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:13.530
Enough does not prove needed.

35
00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:16.490
For each claim, ask which control it needs.
